Z||000006||Spektrum-87GT||10-14-2001||05:50 PM||||v6's are a little harder to work on that most cars, but there is still quite a bit of room(people put v8s in that space!) they dont 'break' a lot, but they're just like any other car from the 80s. remember that most are 15+ years old, its rare to find one that doesnt have a single problem. Also, they dont have power steering, which is not as big of a deal as you'd think. its a little harder to steer at low speeds, but not noticible at higher speeds. personally, i love the no power steering! couple that with a stick and you're in total control  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> so, fieros make great first cars(v6 if you have a little self control). But if you're used to driving around something like a neon(forward wheel drive low power car), they take some getting used to.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88CoupeV6||10-14-2001||06:01 PM||||I think they make great 1st cars but, You may drive it too fast. It is easy to do. Also you should get good experience so you don't get in any accidents. Hope you will not try stupid stuff like street racing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I would hate to see a hurt Fiero.<P>I think they are easy to work on. I am good with working on cars though. To me Fieros are simple and Nice to work on. <P><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig.jpg">    <BR>:D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000008||Mach10||10-14-2001||06:06 PM||||You know, I have MUCH more trouble working on my friend's F150 than my Fiero. There's no more trouble working on a fiero than any other FWD car. All you need is a large 3/4 chunk of plywood covering the trunk to kneel on. If you don't have  arust problem, you can stand in the trunk too. Everything's in a logical place, and not THAT hard to reach. Occasionally you DO have to switch from above and below the car. <P>I'd take a Fiero as a first car in a heartbeat. It's my second, and is giving me better luck than my newer, more expensive first car ever did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000009||WhiteFieroBlur||10-14-2001||06:25 PM||||I have an 87 gt and its my first car ever. Now if you know about cars its like the perfect first car to have cause you can do anything to it. but if your like me and can't even change the oil by myself cause I have not a single tool but a screw driver. or any mechanical skills what so ever. then you might be forking over alot of money in keeping it up. I payed 2500 for it and not even had it over 2 years and I have about put that much into just keeping it running and looking nice. I have to take it to the shop and pay whatever they say to keep it going. And the problems never seem to stop till it has all new parts. But I am learning though, thats why I love this forum. Its like my fiero bible. I have wanted to bomb my car plenty of times, and kick my butt for even getting a fiero than a civic or something. I even had to get a second job and kill myself to put money into it for my system and keeping it alive. People called me obsessed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I am like the only guy in my town fixing it up, so I am kinda alone fiero wise. I am in a car club full of hondas with kits and euro type cars and me with my fiero. but I get alot of looks and so much attention from having a fiero. Its a good way to make plenty of friends cause everyone has stories to tell of back in the day when they had one. And I get the occasional mechanic who just does not want to mess with my car and people saying its nice to drive but are peices of crap. But when it comes down to it I would not even trade my fiero in for a brand new car. a brand new car would seem to boring  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000010||85ZainoGT||10-14-2001||06:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batt:<BR><B>zaino, normal;ly id love to buy your fiero, one problem..i live in san jose california....kinda  long drive eh?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>  heh, well my dad lives about 15 minutes from you, it would probably cost about $500+ to ship it there.. up to you!<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||10-14-2001||06:54 PM||||My first "real" car was a 88 coupe. I had a 88 Pontiac LeMans before that, needless to say I didn't like it much and didnt feel safe in it. It drove like crap and was way to slow.<BR>My 88 coupe was great. I think fieros are excellent cars to work on by ones self   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> . If its going to be a first car you should really get a 4cyl. It WILL keep you out of less trouble. Im glad I didnt have the V6 on my first fiero... I would have probably totaled it. The 4's also get good mileage for a poor 1st time driver. The automatic transmissions have allot less problums then the sticks also. Look for a somewhat low mileage fiero and watch out for rust! Fieros wont show rust to badly, but once they rust out they are in trouble   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000012||85 gt fiero||10-14-2001||07:05 PM||||i'm only 16 and have three fieros great to drive, espacilly my 85 gt rally car really fast and a blast to drive. If you can find a fiero with low miles and not a lot of rust i say buy it.    Randy<P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||12
